  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\n\nIsolated showers in the morning will transition to scattered showers\nand isolated thunderstorms in the afternoon, driven by daytime \nheating. Given the location of the upper trough running from sw oh to\nswrn Lake Erie, the non-supercell tornado threat peaks at 21Z over se\nWarren Co, running along I-71 corridor, and jogging nne to ne \nDelaware Co from srn Madison Co. Generally looking for a day with a\nhigher potential for funnel clouds given this parameter. Enough low\nlevel instability and increased moisture will see some of these\nstronger showers develop into discrete thunderstorms with another day\nof heavy rainfall, although much less widespread. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nSunday night will see the surface low track away from the area to the\nne, with the upper low exiting the CWA overnight. A w-e upper ridge\nlays out over nrn OH but another h5 low tracks to the n from the ern\nTN Vly and enters sern CWA Tues night, bringing a chance of showers\nto srn CWA. The low continues tracking n and then interacts with llvl\nmoisture and daytime heating to create more showers and thunderstorms\non Wednesday. While models differ, Thurs and Fri remain an active\nperiod with rounds of showers and thunderstorms, potentially\ncontinuing into next weekend. \n\nTemperatures are forecast to remain slightly below average, with \nhighs only reaching the low to mid 80s given a moist atmosphere and \nmore cloud cover than typically found. Overnight lows in the low to\nmid 60s will warm to the mid-upper 60s Thurs thru Sat night, upper\n60s to near 70 Sun night.",
